                     <author key="person_1701">J. Gadesden</author>
                     <title key="work_4295">Rosa medicinae</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes"><q>J. Grenstede me scripsit</q> (fol. 239v), in
                        the main hand.</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="138X/139X" notBefore="1380" notAfter="1399"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xiv ex.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#b"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_425">Oxford, Oxfordshire. All
                        Souls' College</orgName><p>2o folio <q>illo sanguine</q> identifies the MS.
                        probably with Rosa medicine 2o folio <q>iᵒ sanguine</q> recorded in All
                        Souls catalogues in the Vellum Inventory (s. xv ex., a. d. 1576; see e. g.
                        fol. 25r); possibly no. 12 in T. James' list, a. d. 1600.</p><p>Binding of
                        white leather over boards, perhaps s. xvi; the clasping is like All Souls
                        work. </p><note type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">Cat. 209, 134; inv. fol.
                        25</note></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_1">Exeter, Devon,
                        Cathedral church of St Peter</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card originally
                        highlighted a copy of <title>Rosa medicinae</title> with secundo folio
                           <q>Que fuit</q>, as reported in Oliver, <title>Lives of the Bishops of
                           Exeter</title> (London, 1861), p. 309; the card also suggested a possible
                        identification with Botfield 18 or 22. However, the Oliver reference was
                        later crossed out, and the correct secundo folio for MS 3506 noted (iᵒ
                        sanguine). This secundo folio corresponds to the All Souls copy (see further
                        above), hence the rejection of the Exeter attribution.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q>Caucio magistri Thome Mordon [LLB Cantab.,
                        a. d. 1405] exposita ciste de Derlyngton. pro iij h. in festo sancti
                        Leonardi et habet tria suplementa ysiderum [sic] cum aliis manipulum florum
                        et zonam argenteam</q>.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q>John Woolton</q> and <q>Ricardus
                        Ackworth</q> (s. xvii). Given by John Mongwell, Senior, January,
                     1658.</provenance>
               </history>
